# cargo-fuzz working state — build output, evolving corpus, and crash reproducers are all local.
/target
/corpus/*
/artifacts
/coverage

# ...with an exception, for the FIVE targets whose re-include rules are listed below. They ship a
# committed SEED corpus. Do not restate that count in prose elsewhere — the re-include rules at the
# bottom of this file are the roster, and `git add -An fuzz/corpus` prints exactly what ships.
#
# For FOUR of them the seed is a CORRECTNESS dependency: random bytes cannot reach their parser
# inside a nightly window at all. `oci_layer_zstd` measured cov 759 from a cold start versus cov 1286
# with one real `.tar.zst` seed — a cold target spends its 300 s rediscovering the zstd frame magic
# and never reaches the packer behind it, which is the "reports success while covering nothing" shape
# this whole workflow exists to prevent.
#
# `validator_classify_serial` is the fifth and is NOT in that class: its seed is a SPEED-UP only. The
# property was measured red from a completely empty corpus at libFuzzer's default 4096-byte
# `-max_len`, in ~900k runs (~7 s), because `cap_line` does not merely truncate — it APPENDS
# `"{head}{TRUNCATION_MARKER}{n} bytes elided"`, so a sub-4096-byte log can render PAST the ceiling.
# It is called out because the opposite claim was written down twice during this pass and is false;
# the target is stronger than its seed suggests.
#
# `cargo fuzz run` uses `corpus/<target>/` as its working corpus dir by DEFAULT, so committing seeds
# there needs no workflow flag; everything the fuzzer then discovers stays ignored by the rule above.
#
# The seeds also set libFuzzer's `-max_len`: with no explicit flag it guesses from the largest corpus
# unit, so the 10 KiB tar seed lifts the tar target past the 4 KiB default (eight tar blocks) into
# the whiteout/hardlink/parent-synthesis tail, and the deliberately INCOMPRESSIBLE zstd seed keeps
# that target's ceiling off the 280-byte structured one.
!/corpus/oci_layer_tar/
/corpus/oci_layer_tar/*
!/corpus/oci_layer_tar/seed-*
!/corpus/oci_layer_zstd/
/corpus/oci_layer_zstd/*
!/corpus/oci_layer_zstd/seed-*
!/corpus/validator_classify_serial/
/corpus/validator_classify_serial/*
!/corpus/validator_classify_serial/seed-*
!/corpus/daemon_create_vm_dto/
/corpus/daemon_create_vm_dto/*
!/corpus/daemon_create_vm_dto/seed-*
!/corpus/daemon_bridge_frame/
/corpus/daemon_bridge_frame/*
!/corpus/daemon_bridge_frame/seed-*
# The lock is working state here too, deliberately, against the usual "commit the lock for a crate
# that builds binaries" rule. It first appeared only when the nightly job was repaired — for 41 runs
# nothing ever resolved this crate — and two facts decide it:
#   * `.github/dependabot.yml` covers `/` only, so a committed fuzz lock has nobody to update it; and
#   * cargo-fuzz 0.13.2 has no `--locked` passthrough, so the lock would not be ENFORCED either.
# Committing it would therefore pin stale transitive versions for the one job whose whole purpose is
# finding memory-safety bugs, with no mechanism to refresh them — the opposite of what the root
# workspace's `--locked` + dependabot policy achieves for shipped code. This crate is
# workspace-excluded, ships nothing, and is rebuilt from scratch on an ephemeral runner every night,
# so resolving fresh is both safe and what we want. Revisit if dependabot ever covers /fuzz.
/Cargo.lock
